WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 246: John C. Calhoun: Nullification, the Cast-Iron Man, and the Road to Secession

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:17.360
[Rebecca] Today, our chronicle of the top 50 famous non-president Americans explores one of the most intellectually formidable, polarizing, and tragic minds in American political history.

3
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.000
[Rebecca] John C.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.240
[Rebecca] Calhoun, the castiron man of South Carolina.

5
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:40.320
[George] Born in 1782 in the rugged backcountry of Abbeville District, South Carolina, John Caldwell Calhoun was the son of Patrick Calhoun, an unyielding Scots-Irish frontier patriarch whose family had fought Cherokee Warriors and British redcoats.

6
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:51.680
[George] Patrick Calhoun was a fierce individualist who taught young John that the government that governs best is that which governs least, and that taxation without absolute consent was slavery.

7
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:59.840
[George] Despite working behind a horse-drawn plough until age 18, young Calhoun possessed a razor-sharp, analytical intellect.

8
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:14.080
[George] He graduated with top honors from Yale College in 1804, astonishing New England professors with his cold mathematical logic and dialectical precision, before studying law at the famous Litchfield Law School in Connecticut.

9
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:22.640
[George] Returning to South Carolina, Calhoun established himself at Fort Hill, his beloved plantation in the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains.

10
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.440
[George] Tall, severe, with bushy dark hair that stood straight back from his brow and burning, deep-set grey eyes.

11
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:41.280
[George] Calhoun seemed carved from granite, earning from English visitor Harriet Martineau the famous description: a cast-iron man who looks as if he had never been born and could never be extinguished.

12
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:53.360
[George] In the South Carolina upcountry, Calhoun's Scots-Irish family had suffered bitter losses in the Cherokee Wars, instilling in him a fierce devotion to local community defense against outside aggression.

13
00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:05.400
[George] At Yale, Calhoun had engaged in a famous debate with college president Timothy Dwight, who prophetically told his faculty that young Calhoun possessed the talents to become President of the United States.

14
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:17.400
[George] Elected to Congress in 1810 at the age of 28, Calhoun began his public career not as a Southern sectionalist, but as one of the most ardent, visionary young nationalists in America.

15
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:28.840
[George] Allying with Henry Clay as a leader of the Warhawks, Calhoun rallied the nation into the War of 1812, delivering fiery speeches defending national honor against British naval arrogance.

16
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.520
[George] Following the war, Calhoun championed sweeping federal programs to unify the Republic.

17
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:48.920
[George] He introduced the bill chartering the Second Bank of the United States, supported the Protective Tariff of 1816, and championed federal road and canal construction, famously shouting in the House, Let us conquer space.

18
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:56.040
[George] We are a rapidly growing republic, let us bind the republic together with a perfect system of roads and canals.

19
00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:03.000
[George] In 1817, President James Monroe appointed 35-year-old Calhoun as Secretary of War.

20
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] Calhoun found the War Department in bureaucratic chaos.

21
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:19.240
[George] Within months, he reorganized the United States Army into an efficient, professional modern military establishment, creating the Bureau of Indian Affairs and establishing modern coastal fortifications.

22
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:32.440
[George] As Secretary of War, Calhoun was praised by military officers for his administrative brilliance, establishing standardized army regulations and saving the Federal Treasury hundreds of thousands of dollars in waste.

23
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:44.760
[George] In Monroe's cabinet, Calhoun formed a close personal friendship with John Quincy Adams, who admired Calhoun's intellectual brilliance before slavery drove them into bitter ideological opposition.

24
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:56.960
[George] Calhoun served as vice president of the United States under both John Quincy Adams and Andrew Jackson, one of only two men in American history to serve as vice president under two different presidents.

25
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.920
[George] But during the late 1820s, Calhoun underwent a profound, fateful intellectual metamorphosis.

26
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:16.800
[George] South Carolina was suffering severe economic depression, its exhausted cotton fields yielding smaller harvests while protective federal tariffs raised the prices of manufactured goods imported by Southern planters.

27
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:28.240
[George] When Congress passed the tariff of 1828, denounced by Southerners as the tariff of abominations, South Carolina exploded in anger, threatening to refuse to pay the tax.

28
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:39.760
[George] Calhoun recognized that as the Northern population exploded due to immigration and industrialization, the South would inevitably become a permanent, vulnerable minority in the federal government.

29
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:51.760
[George] To protect Southern slaveholding society from Northern Democratic majorities, Calhoun abandoned his former nationalism and developed an elaborate constitutional theory to defend minority rights.

30
00:04:51.760 --> 00:05:04.240
[George] The tariff of 1828 placed duties of nearly 50% on imported textiles and iron, prompting South Carolina planters to fly flags at half mast and wear homespun cloth in protest.

31
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:17.280
[George] South Carolina's economic decline in the 1820s was exacerbated by soil exhaustion from monoculture cotton farming, creating an anxious planter aristocracy that viewed federal tariffs as economic oppression.

32
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:28.320
[George] In the autumn of 1828, writing in absolute secrecy at Fort Hill, Calhoun composed the South Carolina Exposition and Protest, adopted by the state legislature.

33
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:37.400
[George] In this groundbreaking, radical treatise, Calhoun resurrected and radicalized the Kentucky and Virginia resolutions of Jefferson and Madison.

34
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:48.680
[George] Calhoun argued that the United States Constitution was not created by the American people as a unified national sovereign, but was a compact between sovereign, independent states.

35
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:58.440
[George] Therefore, Calhoun asserted, each individual state retained the ultimate sovereign power to judge whether a federal law exceeded the constitutional compact.

36
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:11.720
[George] If a state determined that Congress had passed an unconstitutional act, the state possessed the sovereign right to nullify the law within its borders until three-quarters of the states amended the Constitution to grant the disputed power.

37
00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:20.360
[George] If the federal government attempted to enforce the nullified law by military force, the state had the ultimate right to secede from the Union.

38
00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:30.520
[George] Calhoun believed that nullification was not a pathway to disunion, but a conservative constitutional break designed to preserve the Union by checking majority tyranny.

39
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:42.520
[George] Calhoun's concept of nullification drew heavily upon the Roman Republic's institution of the plebeian tribune, which possessed the sacred veto power to check the arbitrary decrees of patrician magistrates.

40
00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:54.040
[George] Calhoun's theory of state sovereignty asserted that the states had existed as independent sovereign nations before 1787 and had never surrendered their ultimate sovereignty to the federal government.

41
00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:08.840
[George] The simmering constitutional feud between President Andrew Jackson and Vice President Calhoun erupted into public confrontation on April 13, 1830, at the annual Jefferson Birthday dinner at the Indian Queen Hotel in Washington.

42
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:17.840
[George] Suspecting that Calhoun and his Southern allies intended to use the dinner to promote nullification, Jackson waited for his turn to deliver a toast.

43
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:30.560
[George] Rising to his full height, holding his wine glass aloft and staring directly into Calhoun's eyes, Jackson delivered his thunderous, unyielding challenge: our federal union, it must be preserved.

44
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.560
[George] The room went dead silent.

45
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.480
[George] Calhoun's hand trembled slightly as he raised his glass.

46
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.960
[George] After a pause, the vice president replied with his famous countertoast.

47
00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.560
[George] The Union, next to our liberty, the most dear.

48
00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:52.720
[George] May we all remember that it can only be preserved by respecting the rights of the states and distributing equally the benefit and burden of the Union.

49
00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.200
[George] The personal and political breach between Jackson and Calhoun was permanent.

50
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:09.760
[George] Calhoun resigned the vice presidency in December 1832, the first vice president in American history to do so, and returned to the Senate to lead South Carolina in the nullification crisis.

51
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:21.280
[George] Following his breach with Jackson, Calhoun was ostracized by the administration, while Jackson privately confessed that one of his greatest regrets as president was that he had not hanged Calhoun.

52
00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:28.640
[George] Jackson was so incensed by Calhoun's nullification doctrine that he drafted a proclamation warning South Carolinians.

53
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.360
[George] Disunion by armed force is treason.

54
00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.840
[George] Are you really ready to incur its guilt?

55
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:44.480
[George] In the Senate, Calhoun formulated his masterwork of political theory, Disquisition on Government, articulating the revolutionary concept of the concurrent majority.

56
00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:51.360
[George] Calhoun argued that a numerical majority rule is inherently tyrannical, reducing the minority to servitude.

57
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] True constitutional republicanism required a concurrent majority, giving each major geographic interest a veto over national policy.

58
00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:08.840
[George] But Calhoun applied this sophisticated constitutional theory to a monstrous moral purpose the absolute, permanent defense of black slavery.

59
00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:35.240
[George] In February 1837, in a celebrated Senate speech, Calhoun rejected the founding father's view that slavery was a necessary evil, declaring defiantly, I hold that in the present state of civilization, where two races of different origin, and distinguished by color and other differences, as well as intellectual, are brought together, the relation now existing in the slaveholding states between the two is.

60
00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.840
[George] Instead of an evil, a good, a positive good.

61
00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:49.400
[George] Calhoun became the supreme intellectual architect of Southern white supremacy, arguing that enslaved labor was the natural and stable foundation of civilized society.

62
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:54.440
[George] Warning that emancipation would bring racial warfare and the destruction of the South.

63
00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:05.880
[George] Calhoun's positive good defense of slavery marked a catastrophic moral retreat from the founding generation's hope for gradual emancipation, hardening Southern resistance against all reform.

64
00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:17.160
[George] Calhoun's disquisition on government became a foundational text for political scientists studying constitutional mechanisms to protect minority populations against majoritarian tyranny.

65
00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.680
[George] In March 1850, 68-year-old John C.

66
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:24.440
[George] Calhoun arrived in the Senate for the final drama of his life.

67
00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:34.120
[George] Emaciated, consumed by pulmonary tuberculosis, with sunken cheeks and his hair white as snow, he was too weak to stand or read his own speech.

68
00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:45.760
[George] Wrapped in a heavy flannel blanket, Calhoun sat in his Senate armchair with blazing, burning eyes while his colleague Senator James Mason of Virginia read his final address to a stunned chamber.

69
00:10:45.760 --> 00:11:06.880
[George] Calhoun warned that the cords of the Union were snapping one by one under northern antislavery agitation, predicting with chilling prophetic accuracy that if the South was not granted an equal balance of power and constitutional guarantees for slavery, disunion, civil war, and national catastrophe would inevitably follow within a decade.

70
00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:16.960
[George] Three weeks later, on March 31, 1850, Calhoun died in Washington, murmuring his final words The South, the poor South.

71
00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.120
[George] God knows what will become of her.

72
00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:30.880
[George] Eleven years later, Calhoun's disciples in South Carolina fired upon Fort Sumter, launching the bloody civil war that incinerated the slaveholding society he had spent his life defending.

73
00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:31.840
[George] John C.

74
00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:42.800
[George] Calhoun remains a towering, tragic paradox, an intellectual titan whose philosophical insights on minority rights were forever poisoned by his defense of human bondage.

75
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:45.600
[George] Calhoun's final resting place in Sint.

76
00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:55.040
[George] Phillips' churchyard in Charleston was visited during the Civil War by Confederate soldiers who viewed his marble monument as the spiritual shrine of the Southern nation.

77
00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:07.120
[George] When Calhoun's body was returned to Charleston in April 1850, thousands of citizens lined the harbor docks in mourning, draping the city in black crepe for the departed cast iron man.

78
00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:23.680
[Rebecca] Sam Houston, San Jacinto, The Lone Star Republic, The Cherokee Brother, and The Union Defiant.

79
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:26.160
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

